<!-- MIDDLE: ARTICLE -->
<article class="post-body">

<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section id="s-default" data-code="default">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Uniform Default</div>
<h2>Every Layer Gets the Same Width — by Convention</h2>
<p>Transformers, state-space models, memory-augmented networks — beneath their different token-mixing strategies, all modern LMs share one defining choice: a stack of <em>identical</em> blocks, each with the same MLP intermediate dimension <em>d</em><sub>ff</sub>. Pick a 440M transformer: every one of its 24 layers has an MLP exactly 4096 wide. The total parameter budget is spread uniformly, one equal slice per layer.</p>
<p>This uniformity isn't principled — it's a default inherited from the original 2017 Transformer and never revisited. A single blunt test exposes the problem. Partition the 440M model's 24 layers into three equal groups (early, middle, late) and assign different MLP widths to each, with total parameters held fixed:</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">capacity-allocation experiment — 440M transformer, 24 layers, equal total params</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click a configuration to highlight it</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body" style="min-height:220px">
    <canvas id="alloc-canvas" style="width:100%;height:200px;display:block;cursor:pointer"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>Front-loading capacity in early layers (wider-early) achieves perplexity <strong>15.96</strong> — 0.32 points below the uniform baseline of 16.28. The reverse allocation hurts <em>badly</em>: wider-late lands at <strong>17.29</strong>, a full point above uniform. Same parameter count, same FLOPs — just a different direction of allocation, and the gap is over a point.</p>
<p>The asymmetry signals something real: early and late layers contribute differently, and the architecture should reflect that.</p>
</section>

<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section id="s-novelty" data-code="novelty">
<div class="section-no">02 — Layer Novelty Drops with Depth</div>
<h2>Later MLP Outputs Reinforce, Not Transform</h2>
<p>Why does front-loading help? The paper answers with a direct measurement on GPT-2 checkpoints (124M to 1.5B). For each layer, compute the cosine similarity between the MLP output and the residual stream it's added to:</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">MLP Novelty Metric</div>
  <span class="math-var">ρ<sup>MLP</sup><sub>l</sub></span> = cos(<span class="math-var">ℱ<sub>l</sub>(z<sub>l</sub>)</span>, <span class="math-var">h<sub>l</sub></span>)
  <span class="math-comment" style="margin-left:1rem">   — averaged over tokens</span>
</div>

<p>When <em>ρ ≈ 0</em>, the MLP output is orthogonal to the existing residual — it's writing genuinely new information. When <em>ρ &gt; 0</em>, it's aligned with what's already there — reinforcing a direction the residual already encodes. A rising trend with depth means diminishing novelty.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">ρ<sup>MLP</sup> vs layer depth — GPT-2 family (124M → 1.5B)</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">darker shade = larger model</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body" style="min-height:220px">
    <canvas id="novelty-canvas" style="width:100%;height:220px;display:block"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The pattern is consistent across every GPT-2 scale: both ρ quantities drop to near-zero in the early-middle layers and <em>climb steadily through the second half</em> of the network. MLP Pearson correlations with layer index range from <strong>r = 0.49 to r = 0.71</strong>.</p>
<p>Interpretation: early MLPs are doing the transformative work — writing orthogonal updates that move the residual toward the final prediction. Late MLPs are largely reinforcing content already present. Under uniform capacity, those late layers have just as many parameters as the early ones — but most of that capacity is going to waste.</p>
<p>Tapering fixes this directly: give more capacity where MLP outputs are most orthogonal (early), less where they're aligned (late).</p>
</section>

<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section id="s-taper" data-code="taper">
<div class="section-no">03 — Tapering MLP Width</div>
<h2>Smooth Cosine Decay from Wide-Early to Narrow-Late</h2>
<p>Tapered Language Models (TLMs) replace the constant <em>d</em><sub>ff</sub> with a per-layer width <em>d</em><sub>ff</sub>(l) that decreases monotonically with depth, subject to one constraint: the layer widths must average to the baseline, preserving total parameters <em>and</em> total FLOPs exactly.</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Budget Preservation Constraint</div>
  <span class="math-comment">d_ff(l) decreases monotonically with layer l, AND:</span><br/>
  <span class="math-var">1/L · Σ d_ff(l)</span> = <span class="math-var">d<sup>baseline</sup><sub>ff</sub></span>
  <span class="math-comment" style="margin-left:1rem">   — same total params &amp; FLOPs</span>
</div>

<p>Three smooth decay schedules are compared. The buttons below show the per-layer MLP width profile (bar height = MLP width) for a 12-layer model with ratio 1.5→0.5 and the resulting perplexity on the 440M benchmark:</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">MLP width profile by schedule — 440M transformer, d_start/d_end = 1.5/0.5</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click schedule to switch</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="sched-btns">
      <button class="sched-btn" data-sched="uniform">Uniform</button>
      <button class="sched-btn" data-sched="linear">Linear</button>
      <button class="sched-btn active" data-sched="cosine">Cosine ✓</button>
      <button class="sched-btn" data-sched="sigmoid">Sigmoid</button>
    </div>
    <div class="sched-ppl" id="sched-ppl-display">
      PPL: <strong>14.44</strong> &nbsp;(−1.84 vs uniform 16.28)
    </div>
    <canvas id="taper-canvas" style="width:100%;height:180px;display:block"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The <strong>cosine schedule</strong> wins decisively. It decays slowly near both endpoints (soft plateaus at wide-early and narrow-late) and most steeply around the midpoint, engaging a wider range of intermediate widths than linear or sigmoid. The result: 14.44 PPL — a gap of 1.84 points vs uniform, all for free.</p>
<p>Implementation detail: per-layer widths are rounded to the nearest multiple of 16 for hardware efficiency, with the first and last layers fixed exactly to <em>d</em><sub>start</sub> and <em>d</em><sub>end</sub>. No other hyperparameters change.</p>
</section>

<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section id="s-sweep" data-code="sweep">
<div class="section-no">04 — The Sweet Spot</div>
<h2>A U-Shape Across Taper Ratios</h2>
<p>The schedule choice is cosine — but how aggressively should capacity be redistributed? That's controlled by the ratio <em>d</em><sub>start</sub>/<em>d</em><sub>end</sub>, which sets how much wider early layers are vs late layers. The paper sweeps five ratios:</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">validation perplexity vs taper range — 440M transformer, fixed total params</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">hover dots for exact values</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body" style="min-height:220px">
    <canvas id="sweep-canvas" style="width:100%;height:220px;display:block;cursor:crosshair"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>Both curves trace a clean <strong>U-shape</strong>: perplexity improves as the ratio grows (up to 1.5→0.5), then degrades. At 1.5→0.5, early layers get 1.5× the baseline width, late layers get 0.5×, and the average is exactly 1.0× — perfect budget preservation. Pushing wider (1.75→0.25) starves late layers too aggressively; going narrower (1.25→0.75) leaves the redistribution too mild.</p>
<p>Cosine dominates linear at <em>every</em> ratio — even cosine's worst configuration (1.75→0.25, PPL 15.49) beats linear's best (1.625→0.375, PPL 15.64). The paper fixes cosine 1.5→0.5 across all subsequent experiments as a robust default.</p>

<table class="results-table">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Taper range</th>
      <th>Cosine PPL ↓</th>
      <th>Δ vs uniform</th>
      <th>Linear PPL ↓</th>
      <th>Δ vs uniform</th>
    </tr>
